  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.4 × loss over x, z from -2 to 2. It is steeper in x, so overshoot shows there first: without noise a step is stable only while learning rate < 2/3 in x (< 2 in z).",
    "Batch noise is Gaussian with mean zero added to the slope, with standard deviation 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)). That is 2 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at batch 64. With noise a step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Arrows are drawn 1:1 in bowl units. Orange is the next step: −learning rate × the noisy slope, the way the ball moves. Grey is −learning rate × the true slope. The readout gives the orange length, or 0 once the run has ended.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss goes above 8, the bowl's highest corner (diverged). Above that, path heights are capped on screen. The ball runs at 5 steps per second.",
    "Noise comes from fixed seed 7, so Reset replays the same sequence. 'New run, keep last path' moves to the next noise values and keeps the finished path as the ghost (each path holds up to 40 points)."
  ]
}